# Code of Conduct

## Our Standard

The OVERRIDE community should be a place where people can discuss, experiment, learn, and contribute without harassment.

Contributors are expected to:

* Be respectful and constructive.
* Assume good faith.
* Give and receive technical feedback professionally.
* Keep discussions focused on the project.
* Welcome contributors with different levels of experience.
* Respect other people's time and contributions.

## Unacceptable Behavior

The following behavior is not acceptable:

* Harassment or discrimination
* Personal attacks
* Threats or intimidation
* Deliberate disruption of discussions
* Doxxing or sharing private information
* Sexual harassment
* Trolling intended to target or intimidate others
* Other behavior that would make the community unsafe or hostile

## Technical Discussions

Disagreement about implementation is normal.

When discussing technical decisions:

* Criticize the code or design, not the person.
* Explain the reasoning behind a disagreement.
* Prefer evidence, tests, and reproducible behavior.
* Be willing to change your position when new information appears.

## Enforcement

Project maintainers may remove, edit, or reject contributions and discussions that violate this code of conduct.

Serious or repeated violations may result in temporary or permanent exclusion from project spaces.

## Reporting

If you experience or witness unacceptable behavior, contact the project maintainers privately.

Please provide enough context to understand what happened and, when possible, include relevant links or screenshots.

Reports will be handled as privately as reasonably possible.

## Scope

This code of conduct applies to project repositories, issues, pull requests, discussions, and other spaces officially associated with OVERRIDE.
